From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 12:22:55 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] vrend: sample the planes of a YUV picture packed into one
 image

A guest allocator without host support keeps a YUV picture as the bytes of one
R8 image: the luma rows, then the chroma rows, in the layout Android defines.
The host holds one texture of that image and a guest shader that samples the
luma plane, or either chroma plane, of an imported image of it read the whole
R8 texture each time: luma came back as the picture and its chroma squeezed
into one third of it, and both chroma views read what luma did. Every decoder
that plays into a GL or SurfaceView view showed stripes.

Let the guest tell the renderer what such an image is. A resource created with
VIRGL_RESOURCE_FLAG_YUV_BIPLANAR (luma rows, interleaved chroma rows) or
VIRGL_RESOURCE_FLAG_YUV_TRIPLANAR (luma rows, then two half-stride chroma
planes) and the picture's width in the high half of the flags is checked
against its size, then keeps one texture per plane: R8 for luma, RG8 for
interleaved chroma and R8 for each planar chroma plane. A triplanar image
makes them when the first sampler view of the image is, as the first plane
number the view carries selects its texture, and the blitter's own context
draws the rows a write changed into them, so an image only a codec or the CPU
touches costs nothing. The capability VIRGL_CAP_V2_PACKED_YUV says the
renderer knows the flags; a guest that sends none gets today's resource, and a
renderer without the capability refuses them as it refused every flag it did
not know.

A biplanar image, which is what the hardware decoders' NV12 buffers are, keeps
its pixels only in the planes, which exist from its creation: its R8 texture is
1x1 and only names it. A transfer of any box of it writes or reads the plane
textures its luma rows and its chroma rows fall in, and nothing is drawn from
an image. A chroma box that cuts a pair of bytes in two, or whose guest rows
are an odd number of bytes apart, goes through the whole pairs it touches, so
the half of a pair the box does not carry is kept. The bytes of a row past the
picture's width, or past its last chroma pair, are padding no plane keeps: a
write drops them and a read leaves the guest's bytes there alone. A decoded
frame so costs one copy at the host, not an R8 image, the planes drawn from it
and the staging the busy image made a write into it need.

A use of the image other than a transfer (a surface, a copy region, a blit, a
shader image, a clear, a view of a plane the image does not have, or a
presenter) first gives it the R8 storage it had before this change, filled
from the planes, with one warning, and the image is a plain one from then on.
Playback and the CTS reach none of them. A triplanar image, which is what the
software decoders produce, and an image imported from an EGL image keep the
storage they had.
